::ENG2_U04_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] A student team is creating a one-page digital flyer for students about reducing unwanted lunch trays. Which choice best matches the audience and purpose?{
=A. Use a short headline, one clear research finding, and a simple visual that shows why completing the pre-order form matters.#Correct. This choice combines audience awareness, evidence, and multimedia design.
~B. Copy every paragraph from the report onto the flyer.#Incorrect. A flyer for students needs selected information, not an entire report.
~C. Use only a decorative image with no explanation.#Incorrect. A visual must support the message and evidence.
~D. Write a long formal memo to cafeteria staff.#Incorrect. The assigned audience is students, not cafeteria staff.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Draft text for students: "Failure to comply with pre-order procedures results in inefficient meal allocation." Which revision is most audience-aware?{
~A. Students are failing the lunch system.#Incorrect. This blames the audience and does not improve clarity.
=B. Completing the pre-order form helps staff prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This keeps the meaning accurate while making the message clearer for students.
~C. Lunch allocation inefficiency exists.#Incorrect. This is still vague and too abstract.
~D. Forms are forms, so do them.#Incorrect. This is informal and does not explain the purpose.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which visual would best support the message that pre-order forms can reduce unwanted trays?{
~A. A random picture of a cafeteria table.#Incorrect. A random image does not clearly support the research finding.
~B. A colorful background with no labels.#Incorrect. Decoration alone does not teach the audience.
=C. A simple before-and-after chart comparing unwanted trays before and after the pilot.#Correct. This visual directly supports the research finding.
~D. A photo of unrelated school supplies.#Incorrect. This does not connect to lunch forms or waste reduction.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] A group is preparing to present its publication plan. Which speaker move best supports collaboration?{
~A. One person reads every slide while others stay silent.#Incorrect. This does not show balanced collaboration.
~B. Each person repeats the same information.#Incorrect. Repetition wastes time and weakens the presentation.
~C. The group skips explaining the evidence.#Incorrect. The audience needs to understand why the evidence supports the message.
=D. Each member explains one role, such as evidence, wording, visual design, or final revision.#Correct. This shows clear collaboration and helps the audience follow the plan.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best combines source evidence with a clear student-focused message?{
~A. The report was about lunch stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not communicate evidence.
=B. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays when students used pre-order forms, so the flyer should explain how each completed form helps staff prepare the right meals.#Correct. This connects evidence, purpose, and audience action.
~C. Everyone should care because lunch is important.#Incorrect. This is broad opinion without source evidence.
~D. The flyer should have a picture because pictures are nice.#Incorrect. This does not connect the visual choice to evidence or purpose.
}
